\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  MATH: The Shekel at ILS2.99 to the Dollar Could Be Coming\b0\par\par\uc2 By Vincent Cignarella   \par \uc2 727 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Mai 2013\par 16:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 It's been almost 17 years since the Israeli shekel was quoted with a "2" in front of it against the U.S. dollar, but expect that streak to be broken, possibly soon.\par \par \uc2 Since July, the shekel has been on a blistering pace, up 14.9% against the greenback. From lows of 4.1 to the dollar on July 26 to today's rate of ILS3.5670.\par \par \uc2 The reason for the shekel's popularity -- Israel is an easy sell.\par \par \uc2 If Israel were a new car, the sales pitch might go something like this: the 2013 model sports an \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  gross domestic product forecast of 3.6% growth that is expected to improve to 3.9% in 2014. That pairs nicely with the Israeli central bureau of statistics last inflation rate, which is 1.5% as measured by the consumer price index. Couple that with a 10-year bond yield of 3.5%, and an investor is driving off the lot with a comfortable 2% real rate of return.\par \par \uc2 Fundamentally the economy is sound. Israel is a major exporting nation with the U.S. and euro zone serving as its two biggest trading partners. A steady demand for Israeli goods with an emphasis on technology and pharmaceuticals continues to lift economic growth, while natural gas production beginning off the Mediterranean coastline should further boost prospects. Over time, that production could ease the heavy cost of imported fuel and perhaps one day turn Israel into an energy exporter.\par \par \uc2 About the only thing standing in the shekel's path to a level not seen since October of 1995 is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem appreciation. The possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains worrisome for shekel bulls, with the latest such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  coming just this week.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will need to do much more if it expects to alter the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate. Even when \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  on Thursday lowered Israel's local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating a notch to A+ from AA-, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  barely budged.\par \par \uc2 Israel still maintains an A or better rating from all three major credit rating agencies. Together, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the week and the credit downgrade have done little to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ascent, with the shekel up 10 of the last 11 trading days and falling just 0.1% on the downgrade.\par \par \uc2 Helping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the shekel's increasing viability as a tradable asset. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Israel has moved from obscurity to the world of black box traders and now appears prominently on radar screens of most hedge funds. Daily trading volume is currently between $4.0 billion to $5.0 billion, according to \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2 ; that is double what it was 10 years ago.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely continue to manage the shekel's appreciation. But with fears of a housing bubble limiting interest rate decreases as a tool, the bank is caught in a bind. The dollar below ILS3.00 is just something exporters are going to have to live with.\par \par \uc2 (Vincent Cignarella is a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist/columnist for DJ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trader and co-inventor of The Wall Street Journal Dollar Index, with 30 years experience in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets including as a bank dealer at major money-center commercial banks. He can be reached at vincent.cignarella@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This item was corrected at 11:20 a.m. EDT to restate the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate.\par \par \uc2 3 May 2013 11:19 EDT DJ  Correction To \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MATH Column\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MATH: An Israeli Shekel at $2.99 Could Be Coming," at 10:39 a.m. EDT misstated the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate in the headline, second paragraph and last paragraph. The headline should read: \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MATH: The Shekel at ILS2.99 to the Dollar Could Be Coming. The second sentence of the second paragraph should read: From lows of 4.1 to the dollar on July 26 to today's rate of ILS3.5670. The last paragraph should read: The dollar below ILS3.00 is just something exporters are going to have to live with.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 03, 2013 11:19 ET (15:19 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130503e953004sm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
