\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Israel cenbank buys $200 mln in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  but shekel stays strong\b0\par\par\uc2 364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 2013\par 10:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying, shekel fixing)\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, April 30 (Reuters) - \uc2 The Bank of Israel\uc2  bought an estimated $200 million of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday in a bid to weaken the shekel after it hit a 19-month high, although the move had little effect.\par \par \uc2 With exports comprising 40 percent of Israeli economic activity, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made it clear it will not allow a steep rise in the shekel.\par \par \uc2 A dealer at a large Israeli bank said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bought about $100 million directly from banks and another $100 million through brokers.\par \par \uc2 The buying, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first foray into the market in three weeks, came as dollar-shekel moved below 3.5850. But the moves only nudged the dollar marginally higher, with the shekel fixed at 3.5940, up 0.1 percent from Monday.\par \par \uc2 After the fixing, the dollar weakened back to 3.5840 shekels.\par \par \uc2 Dealers believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may return on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment, unlike after its prior \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it said it had entered the market.\par \par \uc2 The shekel began gaining late last week on the prospects of the government compiling a responsible 2013-2014 budget, expectations that reforms will bolster the economy, the start to natural gas production and \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  reaffirming Israel's 'A' credit rating.\par \par \uc2 Ramzi Gabbay, chairman of the Israeli Export Institute, said Bank of Israel Governor \uc2 Stanley Fischer\uc2  had taken the necessary steps to prevent a further deterioration of the economy.\par \par \uc2 "This decision is particularly significant in light of the trend of congestion that characterised Israeli exports in the first quarter," he said.\par \par \uc2 Exports rose 1 percent in the first quarter year over year, mainly on gains in pharmaceuticals and electronic components. But excluding these sectors they fell 6 percent, Gabbay noted.\par \par \uc2 "The economic crisis in Europe along with a slowdown in the United States and the ongoing erosion of the real exchange rate severely harms profitability and hurts Israel's exports," he said.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Steven Scheer; Editing by Hugh Lawson)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 ISRAEL\b0 \highlight\uc2 -CENTRALBANK/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2   (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | GFN | M   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130503e94u000c7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
