\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Israel FX \highlight\uc2  players eye  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  after shekel hits 1-1/2-yr high\b0\par\par\uc2 345 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 2013\par 16:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, April 29 (Reuters) - Israel's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants began speculating on Monday over another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the shekel in the coming days after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a 1-1/2 year high against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 With exports comprising 40 percent of Israeli economic activity, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made it clear it would not allow a steep rise in the shekel, which would hurt the country's exporters.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Israel\uc2  bought at least $100 million of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month in its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since July 2011 after the dollar broke below 3.60 shekels. The exchange rate has stayed around 3.63 for the last three weeks.\par \par \uc2 But late last week the shekel started to strengthen again. On Monday it rose 0.3 percent and was fixed at 3.5920 per dollar, its strongest level since September 2011, compared with Friday's fix of 3.6040.\par \par \uc2 After the fixing, the shekel gained to 3.5780 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 "If the trend continues tomorrow, then there is a high chance \uc2 the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again," said Tal Zohar, chief executive of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  broker FXCM \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a type of threat. The next one will be much larger," Zohar said, adding that the trend for a stronger shekel remains.\par \par \uc2 He said the prospects of the government compiling a responsible 2013-2014 budget, expectations that government reforms will bolster the economy, the start to natural gas production and \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  last week reaffirming Israel's 'A' credit rating "all add up to pro-shekel in the medium term."\par \par \uc2 Dealers at Israeli banks said flows during Monday's session were light, mostly local customers selling dollars because they need shekels to pay salaries at the start of next month.\par \par \uc2 A stronger euro-dollar was also helping to weaken dollar-shekel, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Steven Scheer; Editing by Hugh Lawson)\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL-SHEKEL/ | ABN | D | GFN | M | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130429e94t000sc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
