\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: \uc2 Bank of Israel\uc2  Buys Dollars to Stem Shekel's Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 392 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2013\par 14:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds reaction from Israel Export Institute)\par \par \uc2 By Sara Toth Stub\par \par \uc2 Special to DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders in London and Tel Aviv said Monday \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2  bought at least a hundred million dollars to stem the recent surge in the shekel.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the first since 2011, happened just as the dollar dipped below ILS3.6 against the shekel, which many see as a threshold. Trading at ILS3.59 before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar rose above ILS3.62 within minutes of the action.\par \par \uc2 Such an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been widely expected in the market since last week.\par \par \uc2 "It looks like \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2  has set a marker in the sand," said Neil Corney, head of markets at Citi's Tel Aviv office.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Corney said the purchase of dollars, estimated to be "in the hundreds of millions," will likely stem speculative flows.\par \par \uc2 He said the shekel's gain was due to the general weakening of the dollar around the world after weak jobs data. Israel's production of natural gas, which began last week, also works to strengthen the shekel because it will allow Israel to spend less on energy imports, reducing the local need for dollars.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 The government-backed \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  Export Institute praised the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday, which it has been calling for since last week.\par \par \uc2 "This measure has proven \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2 's commitments to Israeli exports and economic growth," Ramzi Gabay, chairman of the institute, said in a statement. But he said the institute still remains "deeply concerned" about the shekel and its impact on exports.\par \par \uc2 Last week, heads of Israeli industry and export groups had called on the bank to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying the stronger shekel was hurting the competitiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exports. Exports make up 40% of gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Monday, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  that he hoped the bank didn't need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. "But if there is a need, we will," said Barry Topf, senior adviser to Bank of Israel governor \uc2 Stanley Fischer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At 1252 GMT, the dollar was trading at ILS3.621 against the Israeli shekel, up from ILS3.589 immediately before the action.\par \par \uc2 Write to Sara Toth Stub at realtimedesklondon@dowjones.com [ 08-04-13 1254GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130408e948000k7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
