\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2  Shekel Drops As Traders Brace for  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Risk\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sara Toth Stub   \par \uc2 499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2013\par 18:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Israeli shekel fell Wednesday as traders grew nervous that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from its highest levels in a year and a half.\par \par \uc2 The dollar pushed 0.5% higher against the falling shekel through the day, reaching a high of ILS3.63. This chipped away at the recent smooth upward path of the Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been rallying faster since a new gas field started production this week, reducing the need for local shekel sales to buy dollar-denominated fuel.\par \par \uc2 The shekel's current levels now match the points where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past, making market-watchers jumpy.\par \par \uc2 "We are closer than ever to another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Rony Gitlin, head of spot trading at Bank Leumi Le Israel, one of the country's largest banks.\par \par \uc2 While the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't set a target for the shekel's exchange rate, it maintains a policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in reaction to sudden rises in the shekel that could compromise the country's exports. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  couldn't immediately be reached for comment Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Expectations for an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been running high since the Tamar field, which contains about 9 trillion cubic feet of natural gas, started production Sunday.\par \par \uc2 The gas field is expected to add one percentage point to the growth of Israel's gross domestic product this year. Taking the gas production into consideration, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects the economy to grow 3.8%; without the gas it would grow 2.8%.\par \par \uc2 The natural gas from Tamar will drastically reduce the amount of money the state-owned electric company and other large companies spend on importing fuel. The reduced need to buy foreign energy also contributes to a stronger shekel, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Before the shekel's surge this week, it had already been trading at its strongest points since late 2011, mainly because of Israel's comparatively high benchmark interest rate of 1.75%. Israel has also weathered the global economic slowdown well, with GDP growing about 3% in 2012, as compared with the near-recession levels in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Israel Governor \uc2 Stanley Fischer\uc2  said Tuesday that the government needs to invest some of its natural gas revenues abroad through a sovereign wealth fund to prevent the shekel from getting too strong. Speaking at a press conference, Mr. Fischer said such a fund, approved last year but not yet set up, should be established immediately.\par \par \uc2 Although the government approved the fund, it isn't clear what office would govern it, and whether or not it would be for the revenue from the Tamar field, or for the larger Leviathan field, scheduled to begin production later this decade.\par \par \uc2 "No one knows how that will work," said Jonathan Katz, chief economist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Write to Sara Toth Stub at realtimedesklondon@dowjones.com [ 03-04-13 1659GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130403e943000pa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
