\par\par \uc2 Top Stories\par \b \uc2 Fin Min: 'Happy' With Israeli Shekel's Current Levels\b0\par\par\uc2 Erin McCarthy  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 582 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2012\par 22:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top News & Commentary\par \uc2 DJCOMM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Israel's finance minister Tuesday heralded the recent weakening of the Israeli shekel and a benign inflation outlook, saying they are improving the country's already healthy economy.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli shekel has weakened more than 12% against the dollar from its strongest point in 2011, creating a reprieve for the country's export-focused economy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last year in order to curb the shekel's gains. Currently, however, the dollar trades around ILS3.780 against the shekel; this compares with ILS3.6 in October and ILS3.4 last summer. Israel favors a weaker shekel, as it helps make its economy, which is highly dependent on exports, more competitive.\par \par \uc2 "Now it's weakening a bit," said Finance Minister Yuval Steinitz in an interview with Dow Jones and The Wall Street Journal, adding he's "happy with current levels."\par \par \uc2 Inflation has also been relatively low and should stay around 2% this year, within Israel's target range of between 1% to 3%, Steinitz said.\par \par \uc2 Lower inflation expectations mark a shift from the first half of 2011, when Israel's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  embarked on a series of rate increases to combat inflation, especially in the housing sector. Last August, higher food, housing and other costs spurred tens of thousands of Israelis to take to the streets in demonstrations.\par \par \uc2 Since then, inflation has eased to an annual rate of 2.2% in December 2011 from 3.4% in August 2011. Monday, the Bank of Israel cut its key interest rate by a quarter percentage point to 2.5%, citing the global economic slowdown and reflecting eased price pressures.\par \par \uc2 The finance ministry recently cut its forecast for gross domestic product for 2012, to 3.2% from 4% previously, citing concerns about an economic slowdown in the euro zone as well as slower growth in international trade, which has affected Israeli exports.\par \par \uc2 Steinitz said Europe is "quite certainly" sliding into a recession.\par \par \uc2 To help underpin Israel's economy and improve local infrastructure, Israel aims to boost investment from across the globe and strengthen ties with the fast-growing emerging-market economies of China and India, the minister said. Chinese and other Asian companies have undertaken investments in Israel, while Israel has most recently taken steps to increase trade with India, he added.\par \par \uc2 Israel was also able to tap international markets with a $1.5 billion, 10-year dollar bond Monday, seeing its lowest yield ever on a dollar bond at 4.115%.\par \par \uc2 The successful issuance is "really a token of trust and confidence in the Israeli economy," the minister said.\par \par \uc2 Externally, however, there are several serious risks for Israel's economy, the minister said. The euro-zone sovereign debt crisis could spill over to its economy, especially as about 30% of Israel's exports go to Europe, he added. Europe is also a significant source of investment for Israel.\par \par \uc2 Iran's nuclear program and resulting international tensions also pose significant security risks for Israel and the "western world," Steinitz said. Still, Israel's economy has proven to be "very resilient" in the past, rebounding from security crises and returning to growth.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think the economy is so sensitive to the Iranian threat, but the country is," he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Erin McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2712; erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Prabha Natarajan contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCOMM1120120124e81o001ik\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
