\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-OECD welcomes \uc2 Bank of Israel\uc2  rate cuts, expects more\b0\par\par\uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2011\par 09:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * OECD: Expect key rate to fall to 2.25 pct by early 2012\par \par \uc2 * Public debt burden remains "uncomfortably high"\par \par \uc2 * Says market supervision should not be in finance ministry\par \par \uc2 (Adds OECD, finance min, PM comments, details)\par \par \uc2 By Steven Scheer\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Dec 12 (Reuters) - The OECD on Monday welcomed the \uc2 Bank of Israel\uc2 's move to lower interest rates in response to the deteriorating global economy, which has started to dent Israeli growth, and said it expected rates to fall more by early 2012 before levelling off.\par \par \uc2 Israel's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut its benchmark rate by a half-point to 2.75 percent in the last three months, after implementing 10 rate hikes from August 2009 to May 2011.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Bank of Israel\uc2  has justifiably switched to a loosening stance in its policy rate," the Organisation for Economic Co-\b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Development said in a report it presented to Finance Minister Yuval Steinitz and Bank of Israel Governor \uc2 Stanley Fischer\uc2  at a news conference.\par \par \uc2 The OECD expects the rate to fall to 2.25 percent by early 2012 and hold there until 2013. OECD economist Peter Jarrett said there could even be just one half-point move should the global economy, particularly Europe, slide into recession.\par \par \uc2 A major risk, the OECD said, would be Israel's housing market not achieving a soft landing and further macro-prudential steps being needed to cool housing prices.\par \par \uc2 The organisation also said that public debt remains "uncomfortably high" in relation to gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 Israel's debt burden is around 75 percent of GDP, and the OECD said the country should aim to reduce it to 60 percent in the near term and even lower over time to lower interest payments and create an ample buffer to external shocks.\par \par \uc2 "Political pressure to relax the deficit for 2012 ... in the wake of the tent protests should be strongly resisted," the OECD said, referring to a wave of protests over the summer against the high cost of living. Protest leaders have called for sharply higher social and welfare spending.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu said on Monday that fiscal responsibility was crucial in the current economic environment.\par \par \uc2 "We acted responsibly, are acting responsibly and will continue to act responsibly," Netanyahu told a business conference in Tel Aviv, adding a key government goal was to keep spending under control. "That is difficult, because there are demonstrations, there are demands, there is the media and there are politicians and legislators."\par \par \uc2 Israel has a budget deficit target of 2 percent of gross domestic product for 2012 but the \uc2 Bank of Israel\uc2  expects it to reach 3.2 percent given lower tax income as a result of an expected slowing of economic growth to below 3 percent next year from close to 5 percent in 2011.\par \par \uc2 Total state spending as a share of GDP is about 45 percent, average for the OECD, but civilian spending is among the lowest in the OECD at less than 35 percent, due to Israel's large defence needs.\par \par \uc2 Steinitz said Israel would stick to its spending ceiling so any deviation in the deficit would come on the revenue side.\par \par \uc2 "If there is a new wave of recession (globally), government revenues will go down," Steinitz told Reuters after the news conference. "We are not going to make changes currently to meet the deficit ceiling. But we are eager to stick to the expenditure ceiling. The expenditure ceiling is in our hands."\par \par \uc2 Fischer said that although the OECD is opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it deemed it necessary. But since the shekel has depreciated in recent months, there has been no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in four months, he noted.\par \par \uc2 The OECD recommended that supervision of capital markets, insurance and saving be removed from the Finance Ministry as part of making financial market oversight more independent.\par \par \uc2 It also praised recent reforms in raising tax income on natural gas and oil finds and recommended an independently managed sovereign wealth fund be established to ring-fence revenues.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Tova Cohen)\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL-OECD/ (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111215e7cc000hg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
