\par\par \b \uc2 Tel Aviv Stock Exchange CEO 'More Worried' About Europe Than Egypt\b0\par\par\uc2 583 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2011\par 19:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Tel Aviv Stock Exchange CEO "more worried" about Europe Than Egypt\par \par \uc2 --Europe's debt crisis influencing Israeli markets and economy\par \par \uc2 --Bank of Israel cuts interest rates; debt crisis could weigh on Israel's export-driven economy\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Steven Russolillo and Erin McCarthy \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Tel Aviv Stock Exchange Chief Executive Ester Levanon said she was "more worried" about the impact of Europe's sovereign-debt crisis on Israeli markets than the political turmoil in neighboring Egypt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Everything that happens in Europe does influence Israel," Levanon told Dow Jones Newswires. "Nothing intrinsic has happened to the Israeli economy, but our markets continue following European markets ... I'm more worried about what's happening in Europe rather than Egypt."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors around the globe have been jostled for months by Europe's sovereign-debt crisis. The threat of contagion spreading to some of the Continent's core countries has been one of the key factors that has spooked markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Egypt has been plagued by anti-military protests in the past week, while nearby Syria has come under fire this year's violent crackdowns on protesters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Currently, 30% of Israel's exports go to the euro zone, according to Levanon, with another 6% heading to Eastern Europe, making the country's economic growth highly dependent on developments in Europe and more susceptible to any slowdown.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such concerns were reflected in the Bank of Israel's decision Monday to cut its key interest rate by 25 basis points to 2.75%, following a previous rate cut in September. The bank said the Israeli economy has slowed in recent months and it expects that trend to continue as a result of financial troubles in Europe and the United States.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Europe's debt crisis "is probably going to affect us, and that's why the change [in policy] was announced today," Levanon said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The second rate cut in three months followed several rate increases earlier in the year as Israel struggled with rapid inflation and rising housing prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Global stocks soared on Monday amid reports that Europe's leaders were making progress toward addressing the crisis. But the heightened correlation between how stocks trade in the euro zone and in Israel is a source of consternation, Levanon said, likening recent trading to the environment during the financial crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It was very frustrating during the crisis in 2008," she added. "Our economy was stable, but the world's problems still affected us ... We are doing the right thing, but it's become a global world."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Israel's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the shekel, has been another victim of the global risk-off trade, weakening more than 10% against the dollar since August amid anxious markets. On Monday, the dollar was down about 0.4% against the shekel at ILS3.778, according to CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Israel's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and local companies would likely be comfortable with the dollar trading at or below ILS4.00, Levanon said. She does not, however, expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, even if the dollar strengthens above ILS4.00.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Like many emerging-market central banks, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the past to manage its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's levels in order to support the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Steven Russolillo and Erin McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2180; steven.russolillo@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Brendan Conway, Cynthia Lin and Prabha Natarajan contributed to this report.) [ 11-28-11 1311ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111128e7bs000fe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
