\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Swedish Jan Jobless Higher Than Expected- SHB\b0\par\par\uc2 1,319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2011\par 11:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1035 GMT [Dow Jones] Sweden's January unemployment rate rose more than expected, jumping to 8.2% from 7.4% on a non-adjusted basis, Handelsbanken notes, adding that in seasonally- adjusted terms the rate rose to 7.9% from 7.8%, explained by a bigger-than-expected jump in the labor force. Still, labor market indicators remain super strong and a resumed decline in unemployment will follow next month, the bank says. EUR/SEK trades at 8.7393 from 8.7082 before the data. (ian.edmondson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1032 GMT [Dow Jones] The slower growth in Norway's GDP is not the huge surprise it might have seemed at first glance, says Erik Bruce, Chief Analyst of Economic Research at Nordea. "It was weak compared to consensus, but there are some upwards revisions of previous quarters, most importantly in the third quarter." Bruce notes the quarterly GDP figures are prone to fluctuations and coincidences, and says year-on-year the growth is exactly in line with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's estimates. Therefore, he does not expect the lower-than-expected figure to influence future rate policy decisions. (niclas.rolander@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1029 GMT [Dow Jones] The March US Treasury contract, up 4 ticks at 118.245, continues to stay within a 118.00-119.00 range with all attempts at an upside break thwarted, according to one trader. In Asian trade the cash market remained bid but also in a tight range and with little direction. The 2-year cash yields 0.835%, 5-year yields 2.33% and 10-year 3.595%. In the 10-year cross market space, the US yield spread to bunds is around 1-2 bps tighter at 36.5 bps while the spread against gilts is around 1-1.5 bps tighter at minus 21 bps. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1029 GMT [Dow Jones] GBP/USD prints the day's high of 1.6132 after hawkish comments from MPC member Sentance. However, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Karen Jones says while it trades below 1.6185, the technical outlook favors the downside toward 1.58. Above 1.6185 would open topside risk toward 1.63 where overhead resistance becomes intense, says Jones. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1027 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds and Spanish yield spreads are largely stable after the Spanish auction. Spain sells EUR3.467B of 10-year and 30-year bonds, the midpoint of its indicative range. The bid to cover ratios were lower than the last tap, although the average yield at the 10-year bond was lower than the last sale held in December. March bunds are up 0.21 at 123.28 and the 10-year Spanish/German yield spread is 6 bps wider at 200 bps. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1025 GMT [Dow Jones] The Israeli shekel remains strong despite rising geopolitical tensions in the region, says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . "Strong growth and inflation support higher rates and the shekel," the bank says. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength around the 3.60 area. USD/ILS currently trades at 3.607. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1023 GMT [Dow Jones] Spanish government bonds may well see some post-supply recovery in the euro-zone government bond market, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  strategist David Schnautz, after Spain sold EUR3.467 billion of October 2020 and January 2037-dated bonds. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 , however, looks for a new 15-year government bond to be launched via syndication in the coming weeks, and given that supply outlook, "the performance potential may be more limited than usual.," Schnautz says. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1023 GMT [Dow Jones] European sovereign debt insurance costs fall Thursday, data provider Markit says, amid a steady market backdrop following Wednesday's rally. Spain's five-year CDS spread is 4 bps tighter at 243 bps as it sells a EUR998 million January 2037 bond. Notes the CDS for France, which will also auction a bond today, is 1 bps tighter at 89 bps. Portugal is 7 bps tighter at 445 bps, Ireland is 3 bps tighter at 585 bps, Belgium is 4 bps tighter at 164 bps and the U.K. is 1 bp tighter at 60 bps, according to Markit. Says the financial indexes are showing similar moves, with the Senior Financials index 2.5 bps tighter at 157 bps and the Subordinated Financials index 7 bps tighter at 268 bps. (hannah.benjamin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1023 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR forward premia are lower after weekly food inflation data allayed concerns over possible stringent monetary action by the RBI, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with a broking firm says. The one-year premia are at 2.560 from 2.620 late Tuesday. A dealer with a foreign bank adds, "a flow from a private corporate with interests in oil in the December 2011, January 2012 contract is weighing on premia" as well. He says, the flow may continue throughout the session, keeping premia in check, but a rebound next week looks likely. The strategist tips the one-year premia in a 2.55-2.60 band for the rest of the session. In the week to Feb. 5, the on-year inflation rate for food articles slowed sharply to 11.05% from 13.07% in the previous week. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1022 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR implied volatilities are under pressure as the spot remains stubbornly rangebound and is showing no signs of a breakout in the near-term, says a trader with a foreign bank. The one-month ATMF is at 7.30%/8% from 7.50%/8.20% late Tuesday; Wednesday was a banking holiday. Still, the spot can't stay directionless for ever, so some players may be looking to roll their long volatilities positions into the back-end, the trader says. But the front-end is unlikely to trade beyond 8% for the next few sessions if the spot continues its recent rangebound trend, he adds. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1010 GMT [Dow Jones] The Bank of Israel is expected to remain committed to limiting shekel strength against the dollar particularly if it tightens monetary policy, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . Adds, the chances of the bank delivering a 25 bps hike on Monday are growing, particularly in light of Wednesday's strong GDP reading. Notes shekel strength could be contained in the near-term due to current tensions in the region. USD/ILS currently trades at 3.593. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0954 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares end up 1.4% at 995.57, marking their highest close since Jan. 21, led by heavyweight energy and banking stocks; trade was active ahead of the long weekend, with Thai markets closed Friday for a national holiday. Sentiment has been buoyed by a return of foreign investors on the buy side. "What happened earlier this year was global money that didn't want to be in Southeast Asia was whipped out...now what we've seen coming in is still global money, but it's Asia-based global money," says Andrew Yates, head of sales for international equities at Asia Plus Securities. Next resistance is tipped at 1000, with support at 980. Among the most actively traded shares, Banpu (BANPU.TH) ended up 2.7% at THB760, PTT (PTT.TH) ended 1.5% higher at THB334, \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) ended up 0.9% at THB118, \uc2 PTT Aromatics and Refining\uc2  (PTTAR.TH) ended 1.9% higher at THB40.50, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) ended up 1.4% at THB149.50, and Thai Oil (TOP.TH) ended 2.1% higher at THB74. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 17, 2011 05:35 ET (10:35 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110217e72h002jx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
