\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Shekel jumps; Israel CPI, GDP data point to rate hike\b0\par\par\uc2 563 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 2011\par 11:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Jan CPI up 0.2 pct on month vs f'cast of -0.1 pct\par \par \uc2 * Q4 GDP jumps annualised 7.8 pct, well above expectations\par \par \uc2 * Rate rise now expected on Monday\par \par \uc2 (Adds GDP data, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analyst comment)\par \par \uc2 By Steven Scheer\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Feb 16 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's shekel <ILS=> hit a three-week high against the dollar and bond yields rose on Wednesday after a jump in January inflation and a surge in fourth quarter growth spurred expectations of a rate hike next week.\par \par \uc2 The consumer price index rose 0.2 percent in January from December and 3.6 percent over 12 months -- confounding expectations in a Reuters poll for a 0.1 percent decline on the month and an annual inflation rate of 3.3 percent. [ID:nJES000052]\par \par \uc2 "The consensus was that the Bank of Israel won't raise rates (this month) after raising rates last month," said a bond trader at a large Israeli brokerage. "But with CPI 0.3 percent higher than expectations I would say that more than 50 percent believe rates will go up."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will decide on rates next Monday. It raised its key rate by a quarter point to 2.25 percent on Jan. 24, its first move in four months and the seventh since August 2009.\par \par \uc2 Adding to the rate hike argument, data on Wednesday showed the economy grew by a faster-than-expected 7.8 percent annualised rate in the fourth quarter -- well above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's models of a 4.3 to 4.6 percent pace. [ID:nLDE71F167]\par \par \uc2 "The economy is on an upward trend and it means the Bank of Israel will have to consider more frequent increases to stem the rise in inflation," said Alex Zabezhinsky, chief economist at DS Securities and Investments.\par \par \uc2 The shekel gained to 3.611 per dollar -- its strongest level since Jan. 26 -- from Tuesday's fixing of 3.65 and a low of 3.71 reached earlier this month. It had started to advance after the January CPI data.\par \par \uc2 The shekel had moved near 3.60 during the session, prompting the Bank of Israel to buy tens of millions of shekels worth of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the appreciation, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 Israel's benchmark 10-year bond <ILGOI0120=TA> fell 0.3 percent in price and its yield rose to 5.01 percent from 4.96 on Tuesday. The one-year bond yield <ILGOI0312=TA> rose to 2.95 percent from 2.92 percent.\par \par \uc2 The yield curve has narrowed to 206 basis points from 212 in the past week.\par \par \uc2 Inflation-linked and corporate bond yields fell on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Analysts in recent weeks had already raised their forecasts for Israel's key lending rate to 3.5 percent by the end of 2011, from 3 percent, on rising inflation expectations.\par \par \uc2 "The (CPI) figure is likely to pull short-term rates higher and could prompt a flattening of the Israeli curve," analysts at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is expected to remain above 3 percent for most of 2011. Israel's government has set an annual inflation target of 1 to 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 Israeli share indices closed 1.3 to 1.5 percent higher.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by John Stonestreet)\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL-MARKETS/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110216e72g000nj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
