\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Steps to curb Israel  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  flows undesired-Fischer\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven Scheer   \par \uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2011\par 20:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments, background, byline)\par \par \uc2 HERZLIYA, Israel, Feb 8 (Reuters) - Bank of Israel Governor Stanley Fischer said on Tuesday recent measures taken by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to battle the speculative flow of capital into the country were undesired but necessary.\par \par \uc2 Fischer told a conference the goal of the steps, including imposing a reserve requirement of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivative deals, was meant to relieve pressure on the shekel <ILS=>.\par \par \uc2 "We are undertaking administrative measures to moderate short-term inflows, which have created the undesired effect on our exchange rate," Fischer said. "These controls are undesired and (we) hope they are temporary, but they are needed."\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's supervisor of banks issued an order requiring that local and foreign banks report their balance of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives on a monthly basis, a copy of the order obtained by Reuters showed. For more see[ID:nLDE7161GQ].\par \par \uc2 Last month, the Bank of Israel imposed a reserve requirement on some \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivative deals and said it will require Israelis and foreigners to report on transactions in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and forwards of more than $10 million in one day. [ID:nLDE70J1KN]\par \par \uc2 In December foreigners made swaps worth $76 billion compared with $19 billion by Israelis and up from $59 billion by foreigners alone in November.\par \par \uc2 Foreigners' investment in Bank of Israel bills jumped by $18 billion in 2010 to $28 billion, a level Fischer said "is huge and puts pressure on the exchange rate."\par \par \uc2 The shekel had reached a 27-month peak of 3.52 per dollar in early January and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly to contain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's advance. It bought $2.085 billion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January to bring its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to a record $73.4 billion.\par \par \uc2 The shekel weakened to 3.71 per dollar last week but has since gained back to a rate of 3.65.\par \par \uc2 Fischer, who has raised Israel's benchmark interest rate by 1.75 percentage points since August 2009, said that while there were successful international rules in place to govern global trade, there were no such laws overseeing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets or capital movements.\par \par \uc2 He said it wasn't possible to set rules establishing how much central banks could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets, but he added that some standards for capital movements were needed.\par \par \uc2 "It is important that the IMF is now trying to develop such rules, to figure out what works and what doesn't work when the exchange rate starts to appreciate and ... what measures they can take that are acceptable from the viewpoint of managing the international economy," he told the conference.\par \par \uc2 "Those are rules we have to develop just as we developed rules gradually in the years since the 1950s that produced a global trading system," he added.\par \par \uc2 Fischer rejected the notion of a global "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" that has led to steep appreciation of the shekel and Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has forced central banks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Latin American central banks continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to prevent rapid appreciation in their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which damages exports.\par \par \uc2 Fischer said the U.S. Federal Reserve was not using monetary policy to create a devaluation of the dollar and inflict harm on other countries.\par \par \uc2 "I believe the U.S. is doing what needs to be done for growth. Furthermore, I believe that when the U.S. starts growing more rapidly, the dollar will strengthen," he said, adding European countries are also trying to get out of recession.\par \par \uc2 "This is not to say that there are no countries that use their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a major part of their economic policy. Of course I am referring particularly to China which has kept its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower than the market would want it to be if the government was not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." (Editing by Crispian Balmer and James Dalgleish)\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL-FISCHER/ (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|D|M|ABX|BNX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110208e728001hy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
