\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/Real Time Economics: Israel Minister Crows About Growth\b0\par\par\uc2 522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2011\par 23:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Real Time Economics blog at blogs.wsj.com/economics)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Posted By Brenda Cronin \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Yuval Steinitz, the philosophy scholar-turned-finance minister of Israel, took a victory lap in the U.S. this week, noting that 2010 was "one of the best economic years ever for Israel."\par \par \uc2 In a conversation with The Wall Street Journal in New York, Steinitz said it was too early to predict the long-term effect of the upheaval in Egypt on Israel and the surrounding region. "If the result is more democracy, that's good," he said.\par \par \uc2 The economic ties between Israel and Egypt are "very low profile," he said, noting that, for the moment, there seemed to be "no direct substantial impact" from the instability.\par \par \uc2 Israel's export-oriented economy, after getting off to a strong start early last year, slowed its pace, notching an estimated annual growth rate of 4.2% in 2010. The growth is thanks in part to booming high-technology and tourism industries. Israel also has steadily trimmed its debt to 75% of gross domestic product from 80% in 2009 and is aiming for annual growth between 5% and 6%.\par \par \uc2 The country saw a record year for tourism, notching more than three million visitors in 2010. Among those tourists, 21% came from the U.S. and 15% from Russia. Israel added 120,000 jobs during the year--many in the tourism sector--and hopes to add 50,000 new jobs annually in coming years, Steinitz said.\par \par \uc2 The finance minister credited the recovery in part to a "very resilient" private sector, which held up despite the country's decision to raise taxes during the crisis. A new challenge, he indicated, is navigating "between a rock and hard place" of rising inflation and an appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the past three years, the shekel has risen 15% against the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the shekel from rising. \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  also has enlisted capital controls to contain inflows of capital drawn by the country's 4.5% rate.\par \par \uc2 Steinitz also echoed a refrain currently popular among leaders, noting that Israel is focusing on being a "competitor" on the world stage. However, he said that increased global competition doesn't mean a return of trade barriers and protectionism.\par \par \uc2 Recently, Stanley Fischer, governor of the Bank of Israel noted that, amid an uneven rise in commodity prices around the world, the international cooperation that flowered as nations sought to dig out together from the financial crisis has dwindled.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think there's much hope of international coordination on the issue of how to deal with rises in commodity prices," Fischer said. "Each country is going to do what it thinks best for itself."\par \par \uc2 However, Steinitz said international coordination is a prerequisite for competition. As long as there is "regulated competition," where everyone knows the rules, "then nobody will block it," he said.\par \par \uc2 -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 . [ 02-03-11 1711ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110203e7230010i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
