\par\par \b \uc2 Israel FinMin Steinitz: Egypt Crisis A 'Surprise,' No Plans To Roll Back Capital Controls\b0\par\par\uc2 702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2011\par 19:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 - Egypt Crisis A 'Surprise,' No Plans To Roll Back Capital Controls\par \par \uc2 - Impact Of Egypt Crisis On Local Economy Minimal For Now\par \par \uc2 - Strong Shekel Problem For Israel Exporters\par \par \uc2 - Future Hikes In Euro, US Rates Will Eventually Ease Shekel Pressure\par \par \uc2 - No Plan To Roll Back Capital Controls\par \par \uc2 - Stands By New Tax Regime For Energy Companies\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Bradley Davis \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The turmoil in Egypt caught Israel by "surprise," the country's finance minister said Tuesday, but despite the conflict, the government will not roll back recently implemented capital controls designed to curtail appreciation in the Israeli shekel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Israel Finance Minister Yuval Steinitz said it was too early to determine the outcome of the Egypt crisis, but that for now its impact on Israel's economy had been minimal. Israel has a miniscule trading relationship with Egypt and its other Middle East neighbors, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the crisis were to worsen, triggering the need for increased security measures in Israel, "that might put a burden on our budget," Steinitz said in an exclusive interview with Dow Jones Newswires and The Wall Street Journal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The shekel weakened in the wake of the initial protests against the regime of Egypt's President Hosni Mubarak, but had already been on a weakening path in response to the government's recent implementation of capital controls. Those measures, which involved removing an exemption that foreign investors had for investing in Israeli government bonds, were implemented in response to a stream of so-called hot money making its way to Israel in a chase for returns on its relatively higher interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The capital controls are "around to stay," Steinitz said, as is the recently hammered-out tax regime for energy companies that will develop two giant gas finds off the coast of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to stem shekel strength; the finance minister said he supported such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , because \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is heavily reliant on exports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank had to act against the strong shekel because the loss of competitiveness could have been "disastrous" for a country whose exports account for 50% of gross domestic product, Steinitz said. The country found itself in a "very unique situation" as its economy marches to recovery, while many of its biggest trading partners among developed countries are seeing sluggish growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So far the capital controls have been effective. Although the safe-harbor dollar weakened by about 0.7% against the shekel Tuesday, over the past month the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has appreciated by nearly 4.5%. This went some way to reverse the strong gains of recent years. From early 2007 to the end of 2010, the shekel rose 15% against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With interest rates low in the U.S. and euro zone, investors have turned to fast-growing emerging markets for returns; many emerging markets, including Israel, have weathered the financial crisis in better shape than more-established economies, and are already on the path to normalizing monetary policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Israel's 4.5% growth for 2010 makes it an attractive destination for investors chasing yield, Steinitz said, as do its 2.25% key government rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But those key rates put Israel between a "rock and a hard place," Steinitz said, because as Israel's economy has recovered, it has needed to increase key rates even more to strangle possible inflation pressures--increasing those rates only increases the appeal of the shekel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the U.S. and euro zone increase their key rates, likely over the next year, he said, pressure on the shekel will ease.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, energy companies drilling for gas in the latest large find off the coast of Israel will "of course" pay higher tax in their exploitation of Israel's natural resources, he said, citing the reforms that his Ministry recently introduced to increase the "government's take" from resource exploitation. Previously, he said, Israel received no net royalty revenue from its natural resources.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Bradley Davis, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2654; bradley.davis@dowjones.com [ 02-01-11 1411ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110201e721000cr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
