\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Israel fights shekel strength with reserves move\b0\par\par\uc2 738 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2011\par 14:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * 10 pct reserve required on non-resident derivative deals\par \par \uc2 * Shekel sheds 1.7 pct but rally seen continuing in long run\par \par \uc2 * Move seen helping \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raise interest rates\par \par \uc2 (Adds analysts' quotes, background)\par \par \uc2 By Tova Cohen\par \par \uc2 TEL AVIV, Jan 20 (Reuters) - Israel on Wednesday became the latest country to combat speculative money flows which have strengthened its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it imposed a reserve requirement on some \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivative deals.\par \par \uc2 From Jan. 27, banks in Israel will have to meet a 10 percent reserve requirement on the value of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and forwards transactions by non-residents, the Bank of Israel said, as it seeks to protect exports and contain inflation.\par \par \uc2 "In the last few months the volume of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivative transactions by non-residents has increased markedly," the Bank of Israel said.\par \par \uc2 "A significant part of the increase in non-residents' transactions is in short-term instruments. This measure will strengthen the Bank of Israel's ability to achieve the objectives of its monetary, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and financial stability policies."\par \par \uc2 In December foreigners made swaps worth $76 billion compared with $19 billion by Israelis and up from $59 billion in November.\par \par \uc2 The shekel weakened to 3.62 shekels per dollar from 3.56 prior to the announcement. The Bank of Israel is trying the reduce the amount of speculative capital flows by imposing a liquidity requirement of 10 percent of the value of the transaction, Ron Eichel, chief economist at Meitav Investment House, said.\par \par \uc2 "This can change short-term trends and reduce noise in the market but if the economic fundamentals are strong, in the end the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will strengthen," Eichel told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 "Israel is like Brazil and other countries, it is trying to help exports and growth."\par \par \uc2 Brazil, Chile, Peru, Taiwan, South Korea, Turkey, South Africa and others have taken measures to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening or control the flow of money into their economies as investors pour money into higher-yielding markets.\par \par \uc2 SHEKEL WILL CONTINUE TO GAIN\par \par \uc2 The shekel reached a 27-month high of 3.528 shekels per dollar earlier this month and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly to contain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rally. It bought $2.3 billion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in December, bringing its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to a record $70.9 billion.\par \par \uc2 Eichel said the shekel will continue to strengthen in the long term even without speculators because economic growth is strong, interest rates are expected to rise and there is a surplus in the country's balance of payments. Israel's economy grew a provisional 4.5 percent in 2010.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think anyone in the Bank of Israel is innocent enough to believe this will change the trend," Eichel said.\par \par \uc2 Fourteen of 15 economists polled by Reuters expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise its short-term lending rate by 0.25 percentage point to 2.25 percent on Monday. The rate is expected to reach 3.0-3.5 percent by the end of 2011.\par \par \uc2 Michael Sarel, head of economic research at Harel Finance, said even a short-term depreciation of the shekel will enable the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates more rapidly.\par \par \uc2 "We estimate that in the wake of the Bank of Israel's measure, the chances for a rate hike on Monday have increased considerably and the rate of increases in the interest rate in the coming year is expected to be more rapid," Sarel said.\par \par \uc2 A Bank of Israel source said the reserves measure will make this type of transaction less attractive for foreign players.\par \par \uc2 "It makes the transaction more expensive for local banks," the source said, as the banks will not get interest on the 10 percent reserves and this will carry over to foreign banks.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Israel's order follows its announcement on Wednesday that it will require Israelis and foreigners to report on transactions in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and forwards of more than $10 million in one day.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, non-residents who perform transactions in short-term Bank of Israel bills known as makams and short-term government bonds of more than 10 million shekels in one day will be required to report details of the transactions and their balance of holdings of such assets.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Toby Chopra)\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /DERIVATIVES (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110120e71k001ga\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
