\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Shekel slides on aggressive Israel cbank buying\b0\par\par\uc2 607 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2011\par 15:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys $450 million of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  -dealers\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week estimated at $1 billion\par \par \uc2 * Euro-shekel moves off 8-1/2 year low\par \par \uc2 * December inflation data due on Friday\par \par \uc2 By Steven Scheer\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Jan 13 (Reuters) - Israel's shekel slid against the euro <EURILS=> and dollar <ILS=> on Thursday, as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  spiked globally and on another day of aggressive Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The combination of the two sent the shekel sliding more than 3 percent against the euro, a day after the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit an 8-1/2 year low against the Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dealers estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought some $450 million of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in three rounds during the day as it continues to fight powerful market forces in trying to prevent a rapid appreciation of the shekel, which risks hurting Israeli exports.\par \par \uc2 One dealer at a large Israeli bank said the Bank of Israel had bought around $1 billion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week. That compared with purchases of $1.82 billion for all of December.\par \par \uc2 On most days, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has marginal, if any, impact on daily trading but dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  caught the market off-guard on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "The Bank of Israel understood the market was short dollars and it really had an impact," said the dealer, who asked not to be named. "When the market is short, you don't need a lot of dollar buying ... It was a very crazy day."\par \par \uc2 Last week, the shekel had reached 3.5280 per dollar, its strongest level since October 2007. On Wednesday, it hit 4.5855 per euro, its highest point since August 2002.\par \par \uc2 "The Bank of Israel is defending the 3.50 per dollar and 4.60 per euro levels strongly," the dealer said.\par \par \uc2 At 1330 GMT, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the shekel at 3.5660 to the dollar and 4.7052 per euro, up 0.8 and 2.6 percent, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said that buying by hedge funds and foreign banks of a few hundred million dollars helped to push the dollar up to near 3.59 shekels and the euro to 4.76 after the fixing, before profit taking set in and pared those gains late in the session.\par \par \uc2 Also boosting euro-shekel was another strong rally in euro-dollar to above $1.33.\par \par \uc2 Another dealer at an Israeli bank said there was talk of the Finance Ministry buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but the ministry said it was just a regular \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of swapping dollar-denominated debt into shekels.\par \par \uc2 Last week, a ministry official said the ministry planned to swap a few billion dollars of its debt in 2011 to take advantage of the shekel's strength. [ID:nLDE7040X3]\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the trend is for the shekel to further appreciate due to a strong economy and expectations of a few more interest rate increases this year that will further widen the gap with the United States and Europe and encourage more capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Israel has raised its benchmark rate by 1.5 percentage points to 2 percent since August 2009 but it left the rate unchanged at its past three meetings. Still, the market believes the rate will rise to 3 percent or more this year.\par \par \uc2 The timing depends on the inflation outlook. Inflation data from December is due on Friday, with the consumer price index forecast to have risen 0.3 percent from November and 2.6 percent over the past 12 months. [ID:nLDE7090NX] (Editing by Susan Fenton)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 ISRAEL\b0 \highlight\uc2 -SHEKEL/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110113e71d00196\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
