\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Israel cenbank to keep buying  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  -Fischer\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven Scheer   \par \uc2 435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 2010\par 15:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments)\par \par \uc2 TEL AVIV, Dec 13 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to ensure Israeli exports remain competitive, Bank of Israel Governor Stanley Fischer said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts have questioned whether Fischer can continue contradictory policies of buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  while also raising interest rates, especially with the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves nearing $70 billion.\par \par \uc2 Israel's shekel <ILS=> stands at a near two-year high against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "One of the threats to (economic) growth is the exchange rate," Fischer told a business conference, noting that exports comprise 45 percent of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic activity. "We will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as needed."\par \par \uc2 Fischer has increased \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark rate by 1.5 percentage points to 2 percent since August 2009, although rates have been left unchanged for the past two months amid signs of slowing economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The rate hikes have helped strengthen the shekel, which in turn has led the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy tens of billions of dollars to curb its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains.\par \par \uc2 Fischer also said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not allow surging housing prices to turn into a real estate bubble and that the Bank of Israel would continue to take steps if prices did not fall.\par \par \uc2 He said inflation will likely end 2010 within an annual government target of 1-3 percent. The rate was 2.5 percent in October.\par \par \uc2 Fischer said Israel's economy, which is on track to grow 4 percent in 2010, is in a good situation. He downplayed a rise in the unemployment rate to 6.6 percent in the third quarter from 6.4 percent in the previous three months -- near an all-time low of 5.9 percent -- saying the gain was due to a jump in the labour participation rate.\par \par \uc2 Also at the conference, Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu said Israel must stick to responsible fiscal policies.\par \par \uc2 "Keeping responsible fiscal policies is critical," he said, adding he intended for Israel to become one of the world's top 15 economic powers in the next 15 years.\par \par \uc2 "We have to lower, restrain or reduce spending. We have to lower taxes. We have to invest in education. We have to expand infrastructure and we have to encourage entrepreneurship and cut bureaucracy," he said.\par \par \uc2 If Israel grew an additional 1.5 percentage points a year, Israel could reach that target, Netanyahu said. (Reporting by Steven Scheer; Editing by Hugh Lawson)\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL-FISCHER/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|MD|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101213e6cd0010g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
