\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-OECD ups most 2010 GDP fcasts for E.Europe, Israel\b0\par\par\uc2 716 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2010\par 11:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 PRAGUE, Nov 18 (Reuters) - The OECD raised its 2010 growth forecasts for most its members in emerging Europe on Thursday, although it reduced that of Hungary and tempered its 2011 outlook for Israel, the Czech Republic and non-member Estonia.\par \par \uc2 The Organisation for Economic Cooperation and Development said Polish inflation indicated monetary tightening ahead and \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should continue raising rates, although \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  there were increasingly unwarranted.\par \par \uc2 Following are the main points from the OECD Economic Outlook report on OECD members including Israel and ex-communist European Union states.\par \par \uc2 POLAND Poland is seen growing 3.5 percent this year and 4 percent in 2011, helped by booming exports, a surge in domestic demand, infrastructure investments partly funded by the EU, and preparations for the 2012 Euro football Championship.\par \par \uc2 The OECD said inflation was expected to rise, "pointing to the need for an early start to monetary tightening". Many analysts believe Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may raise its 3.5 percent base rate at a meeting next week.\par \par \uc2 Poland's fiscal deficit is seen rising to 8 percent of economic output this year, well above the 6.9 percent originally planned, and should decline to 6.7 percent of GDP next year.\par \par \uc2 The OECD said plans to cap public spending and hike taxes, twinned with strong growth, should put public finances on a more sustainable path in 2012, but it said Poland's request that it and other new EU members be allowed to exclude spending tied to pension reform would jeopardise fiscal consolidation if granted.\par \par \uc2 CZECH REPUBLIC The OECD expects the Czech economy to grow by 2.4 percent in 2010 and 2.8 percent in 2011, driven by exports to its main trading partner Germany as a weak labour market and austerity steps by the right-of-centre government curb domestic demand.\par \par \uc2 It said growth should accelerate to 3.2 percent in 2012, with the main risks being developments in major export markets, while consumers could also reduce activity due to austerity and an expected increase in electricity prices next year.\par \par \uc2 It said Czech monetary policy can remain accomodative for some time because, even though headline inflation has picked up to near the centre of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range, fiscal consolidation would tame price pressure.\par \par \uc2 HUNGARY The OECD trimmed its 2010 growth forecast to 1.1 percent, from 1.2 percent previously. It saw Hungary's expansion quickening to 3.1 percent in 2012 on the back of a strong German recovery, while inflation should stabilise at around the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 3 percent target, the OECD said.\par \par \uc2 It said that after the government passed temporary measures to stabilise finances, including ad-hoc taxes on banks and mostly foreign-owned utilities and retailers and a decision to roll back pension reform, it needed to spell out a credible, long-term fiscal plan to avoid losing investor confidence.\par \par \uc2 The OECD said downside risks could be compounded if Budapset does not reach a precautionary safety net agreement with international lenders following the expiration of a 20 billion euro International Monetary Fund-led deal that ended last month.\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL\par \par \uc2 Israel's economy is expected to slow slightly in the second half of this year for full-year growth growth of 3.9 percent, inching up 4 percent or more in 2011 and 2012.\par \par \uc2 The OECD said inflation was likely to trend towards the upper limit of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 1 to 3 percent target band and therefore policymakers needed to continue raising interest rates. It also said foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases meant to shield the export sector were "increasingly unwarranted".\par \par \uc2 ESTONIA\par \par \uc2 The OECD said a return to robust consumer spending would drive growth to 3.4 percent next year and about 4 percent in 2012. It said headline inflation was "worrying", although persistent double-digit unemployment and general economic weakness meant core price growth would pick up more slowly.\par \par \uc2 It said fiscal policy was under control and the fiscal gap was not expected to exceed the EU's prescribed ceiling of 3 percent of gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Michael Winfrey; Editing by John Stonestreet)\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-OECD/EASTEUROPE (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|HX|PX|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101118e6bi000m8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
