\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(10/20)  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2 's Bank Chief Stands By  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 564 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2010\par 02:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A16\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL) \par    By Charles Levinson \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JERUSALEM -- \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's influential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief Stanley Fischer defended his aggressive attempts to keep a lid on the shekel, saying his long-running \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has successfully protected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the worst of the global economic and financial crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 His defense, in an interview at the Bank of Israel here, comes as other countries, including Japan and Brazil, have more recently moved to keep their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in check to stimulate exports and, by extension, broader economic growth.\par \par \uc2 That has roiled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent weeks, triggering a debate on the need for new global accords to better regulate exchange-rate policy. Mr. Fischer's weak-shekel policy predates the current turmoil, and hasn't generated much fuss because of the relatively small size of Israel's economy.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Fischer commands an outsized voice in global financial markets, in part because of his role tamping down the Asian crisis in the late 1990s, when he was the International Monetary Fund's second-in-command.\par \par \uc2 "The prime reason we're continuing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  is we want to protect, we want to not penalize the export sector excessively" from exaggerated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations, he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fischer, meanwhile, has steadily raised interest rates amid a real-estate boom -- adding upward pressure on the shekel by luring investors.\par \par \uc2 He shrugged off the competing challenges. "It's rare in central banking that you're not engaged in an act of trying to balance various conflicting factors," he said.\par \par \uc2 Israel's challenge is similar to that of many other export-dependent economies that have struggled to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  relatively weak and their exports competitive as global investors -- in search of higher yields -- pour money into their markets.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fischer began buying up dollars in March 2008, the first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in over a decade. The global financial crisis was starting to snowball, and the buying was aimed at bolstering Israel's foreign reserves for tough times ahead, as well as depressing the shekel to help ailing exporters, who account for between 40% and 45% of Israel's economic output.\par \par \uc2 From mid-2008 to mid-2009, the bank snatched up $100 million a day. It has continued at a furious pace, adding over $7 billion to its reserves so far this year. In mid-2008, the dollar was fetching about three shekels; through most of 2010, it was buying just under four shekels. It has fallen back again to around 3.6 shekels in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 The dollar buying equals roughly 3% of gross domestic product. Foreign reserves stand at nearly $70 billion, up from $29 billion before Mr. Fischer began his dollar binge.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fischer has faced criticism for his policy. The Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development has urged a halt. Mr. Fischer's two predecessors and Finance Minister Yuval Steinitz have publicly questioned the policy.\par \par \uc2 Carrying large dollar reserves when U.S. interest rates are at or near zero is also expensive. Mr. Fischer said broader economic concerns outweigh any losses from interest-rate gaps.\par \par \uc2 His policy, meanwhile, appears to have helped Israeli exports, which bottomed out in the first quarter of 2009, but have steadily rebounded.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 19, 2010 20:27 ET (00:27 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101020e6ak00013\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
