\par\par \b \uc2 Israel FinMin urges c. bank not to raise s-term rates\b0\par\par\uc2 422 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2010\par 18:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Oct 12 (Reuters) - Israeli Finance Minister Yuval Steinitz on Tuesday again urged Bank of Israel Governor Stanley Fischer not to raise short-term interest rates, saying a widening rate gap with Western countries was pushing the dollar down against the shekel.\par \par \uc2 Both Steinitz and Fischer have warned repeatedly over the past two years that a strong shekel is hurting Israel's exports.\par \par \uc2 "We need to keep reasonable gaps between us and the world because when the interest rate gap with the United States widens, dollars search for a place to go, and go to the place where rates are the highest," Steinitz told a conference.\par \par \uc2 "We need to be careful not to raise rates too much in comparison with Western countries," he added.\par \par \uc2 Steinitz, in a meeting with ministry officials, had also requested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  leave rates unchanged last month.\par \par \uc2 But the Bank of Israel on Sept. 27 raised its benchmark rate by a quarter-point to 2 percent, compared with zero for the United States and 1 percent for the euro zone. It was the sixth move since August 2009 and comes as surging housing prices threaten higher inflation.\par \par \uc2 The higher rates, coupled with a stronger euro-dollar, have led to a sharp appreciation of the shekel <ILS=> against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a two-year high, hurting the export sector which accounts for more than 40 percent of Israel's economic activity.\par \par \uc2 To stem the dollar's slide, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market by buying hundreds of millions of dollars in recent weeks as the shekel rose from 3.85 per dollar in late July to 3.60.\par \par \uc2 Steinitz, who believes exporters should diversify more to rapidly-expanding markets in Asia, said he supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He said his relationship with Fischer was good, even though the two do not always agree on policy.\par \par \uc2 Steinitz also said the problem of Israel's high housing prices could not be solved by raising interest rates or taxes, but instead with more supply of homes. The problem would not be resolved for "a year or two", he said.\par \par \uc2 On the economy, Steinitz said that despite growth expected to top 4 percent this year, "we have not returned to normal" since weakeness in the United States and Europe would affect Israel. (Reporting by Steven Scheer; Editing by Susan Fenton)\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL-FINMIN/|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|MD|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101012e6ac0016r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
