\par\par \b \uc2 Israel shekel at 2-year peak vs dollar, rate rise seen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven Scheer   \par \uc2 343 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 15:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Sept 29 (Reuters) - The Israeli shekel <ILS=> rose 0.3 percent to a nearly two-year high against the dollar in light holiday trading on Wednesday on expectations of further Israeli rate rises and global dollar weakness.\par \par \uc2 The shekel was fixed at 3.6650 per dollar -- its strongest level since Oct. 15, 2008 -- from Tuesday's rate of 3.6770, which had been an 8-1/2-month peak.\par \par \uc2 The fix took place three hours earlier than usual because of the Jewish festival of Sukkoth, which ends on Thursday evening. Trading volumes were very low and volatile. Financial markets will be closed on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  said in a client note that it expects the shekel to strengthen further to 3.60 now that it has broken above 3.67.\par \par \uc2 The shekel's advance from a low of 3.85 began after the Bank of Israel surprised the market with a rate rise in late July. The benchmark rate was held in August, but the shekel rose further in September on expectations of a rate hike this week.\par \par \uc2 As expected, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its key rate by a quarter-point to 2 percent on Monday, its sixth such move since August 2009, in a bid to damp down surging real estate prices and rising inflation.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect one more hike by the end of the year, and further rises in the key rate to as much as 3.5 percent in 2011.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's recent global weakness has been exacerbated by expectations of further easing measures by the Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently in the past month to prevent a rapid appreciation of the shekel, which would hurt exports. It bought an estimated $250 million of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the open market on Tuesday, but dealers did not report any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday. (Editing by Tim Pearce)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 ISRAEL\b0 \highlight\uc2 -SHEKEL/|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|RBN|MD|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100929e69t000wt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
