\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-UPDATE 1-Low global rates bar 'normal' Israel rates\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven Scheer   \par \uc2 929 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2010\par 14:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds Stanley Fischer comments)\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, July 12 (Reuters) - Israeli interest rates cannot revert to "normal" levels as long as U.S. and European rates remain low, Bank of Israel Governor Stanley Fischer said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its key lending rate by 1 percentage point between August and March as Israel's economy rebounded from a downturn and as annual inflation remained above an official target of 1-3 percent.\par \par \uc2 Policymakers seek to return the key rate, currently at 1.5 percent, to a more "normal" level that reflects the economic environment. Yet rates have been on hold for the past three months as inflation has eased and the European debt crisis has increased uncertainty over the global economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 While Fischer did not define "normal", Israel's benchmark rate stood at 4.25 percent in September 2008 before the global financial crisis deepened.\par \par \uc2 "We can't go back to 'normal' if the Federal Reserve is at 0-25 basis points and the ECB is at 1 percent," Fischer told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 He noted that further interest rate increases while global rates were still low would mean Israel would have to "absorb large amounts of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that would make policy more complicated".\par \par \uc2 More foreign inflows would lead to a stronger shekel <ILS=> <EURILS=>, which the central back opposes since it would hurt exports and the economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 "We don't have to go back to normal if the rest of the world doesn't go back to normal," Fischer said.\par \par \uc2 U.S. and European rates had been expected to rise by now.\par \par \uc2 "But it seems pretty clear that they will be delayed," Fischer said, adding that the expectation was that Fed and ECB rates would start going up about now. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  believes global rates will stay on hold for the rest of 2010.\par \par \uc2 "We require a careful balance in continuing to keep the economy going and ... keeping inflation in the target range," Fischer added. "We can't let inflation get out of control -- which is above 3 percent."\par \par \uc2 Inflation -- at an annual rate of 3 percent in May -- is expected to ease to 2 percent in a few months before returning to near 3 percent the next 12 months.\par \par \uc2 Analysts have scaled back expectations of interest rate hikes. In minutes of the June 28 rates decision, the Bank of Israel said forecasters expect rate hikes of 1 to 1.5 points in the next 12 months.\par \par \uc2 HIGH HOUSING COSTS\par \par \uc2 Fischer said the main inflation concerns are high housing prices and indirect tax increases. House prices, which are some 21 percent of the CPI, rose 4.8 percent in May year-on-year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently imposed fees on banks to try and cool morgage lending. Fischer said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would wait and see if that helped to lower housing prices before enacting additional steps.\par \par \uc2 Fischer in May started a second term as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief. When asked whether he planned to stay the entire five years, Fischer said much depended "on how the second term goes" and how much he achieves. "I am happy with what I am doing," he said.\par \par \uc2 One key goal is to implement a new Bank of Israel law that includes a monetary board to set interest rates. At present, the governor decides on rates after discussions with bank officials. The government still needs to nominate candidates to the board.\par \par \uc2 "I hope to see the board soon," said Fischer, saying another goal for his second term was integrating Israeli Arabs and ultra-Orthodox Jews into the workforce, which would boost Israel's economy.\par \par \uc2 Fischer said Israel's economy is showing a host of mixed signals -- a 3.6 percent annualised rise in first quarter economic growth and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  companies report indicating continued expansion in the second quarter but expectations for weaker exports later in the third quarter, and a drop in the May purchasing managers' index.\par \par \uc2 He said there was no reason for the Bank of Israel to change growth forecasts of 3.7 percent in 2010 and 4 percent in 2011.\par \par \uc2 Still, a stronger shekel versus the euro should weigh on exports to Europe, he said, although the shekel has dipped against the euro recently.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of the pessimism a month ago for the second half of the year has moderated of late," Fischer said, citing the IMF raising global growth estimates.\par \par \uc2 "For (Israeli) exporters, it doesn't as bad as it did 10 days ago" when the euro was very weak, he said.\par \par \uc2 On fiscal policy, Fischer said he supported a rise in annual budgetary spending to 2.66 percent the next two years from a 1.7 percent ceiling in place the prior few years.\par \par \uc2 Such spending levels continue to ensure funding for education and vital programmes while also pushing the debt-GDP ratio lower, as long as the economy continues to grow.\par \par \uc2 The ratio is about 80 percent and Fischer expects a 1-2 percent a year decline in the next few years.\par \par \uc2 Fischer also said that while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not issue a formal announcement that it will stop buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he hopes it will not be necessary. The bank has not bought foreigh exchange since May.\par \par \uc2 "If the market goes back to some normality, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  ... will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less," he said.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Ron Askew)\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL-FISCHER/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|MD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100712e67c000wh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
