\par\par \b \uc2 DJ OECD:  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2  Must Cut Public Debt, Stop  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sara Toth Stub  \par \uc2 736 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2010\par 12:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy has weathered the recent economic downturn better than most, but faces major challenges in reducing its public sector debt and dealing with high poverty rates among its minority populations, the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development said in its first-ever review of Israel's economy.\par \par \uc2 The report called on the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by purchasing dollars, and said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are more than adequate.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, the bank has been purchasing dollars to prevent the shekel from gaining too much strength against the U.S dollar, and in turn, making Israel's exports more expensive. Such a policy is not sustainable for the long term, OECD secretary general Angel Gurria said.\par \par \uc2 Israel is near the end of a three-year long accession process to become a member of the OECD, and OECD officials are in Jerusalem this week presenting the review and meeting with Israeli officials.\par \par \uc2 Gurria said Israel is "in the home stretch" of being accepted to the OECD, and is expected to have membership by May. Membership of the group would cement Israel's status as a developed economy. Earlier this month Chile joined the organization, becoming its thirty-first member.\par \par \uc2 Although the OECD report highlights problematic issues in Israel's society and economy, Israel's admission to the OECD is not contingent on solving these problems or implementing OECD recommendations.\par \par \uc2 The review focused much attention on the low employment and high poverty rates among Israeli Arabs and ultra-Orthodox Jews, which together make up 28% of the population.\par \par \uc2 About 20% of Israeli households live below the relative poverty line, compared to an average of 11% for the OECD countries, the report said. This is mainly due to high rates of poverty and low rates of labor-force participation among Israeli Arabs, which make up 20% of the population, and among ultra-Orthodox Jews, which make up 8% of the population.\par \par \uc2 The report encouraged Israel to invest in increasing educational and employment options for those groups.\par \par \uc2 Avishay Braverman, Israel's minister of minority affairs, said belonging to the OECD will make Israel more accountable in how it treats its minorities and will help spur efforts to create more equal opportunities for Arab and ultra-Orthodox Jewish Israelis.\par \par \uc2 "The importance is, when you join a prestigious club, you have to act according to the club," Braverman said.\par \par \uc2 "The message we get from the report is that there are solutions to our problems," said Stanley Fischer, governor of the Bank of Israel, at a symposium in Jerusalem Wednesday, which was attended by OECD officials and Israeli government officials.\par \par \uc2 Israel's economy grew by 0.5% in 2009, while many other developed economies around the world contracted. Gurria said the OECD expects Israel's GDP to increase at least 3% in 2010, and 4% in 2011.\par \par \uc2 Gurria said Israel has much to gain from joining the OECD, including increased foreign investment. But perhaps more importantly, belonging to the OECD will allow Israelis to mix with social and economic experts from other member states, and will give Israel access to policy recommendations and reviews from the OECD.\par \par \uc2 Israel, with its booming high-tech industry and high number of startup companies can also contribute knowledge to other member states, he said.\par \par \uc2 "We are going to learn from Israel and Israel is going to learn from the member states," Gurria said.\par \par \uc2 In many categories, Israel lags behind OECD countries. Israel's public sector debt is likely to be 82% of gross domestic product in 2010, much above the 60% that is the target for OECD member countries.\par \par \uc2 The high level of debt is due in part to high spending on defense. The report said that Israel's spending on defense was equal to about 8% of annual GDP, whereas the OECD average for defense spending is around 2% of GDP.\par \par \uc2 The report called for increasing the sales tax to help limit the debt, and urged Israel to rethink the exemption from sales tax for fruits and vegetable and on all products and services in its southern tourist city of Eilat.\par \par \uc2 By Sara Toth Stub, contributing to Dow Jones Newswires; saratoth@gmail.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 20, 2010 06:09 ET (11:09 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100120e61k0027l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
