\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-OECD calls for  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2  to end  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven Scheer   \par \uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2010\par 07:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from OECD, Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Jan 20 (Reuters) - Israel's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should stop buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "as soon as possible", saying such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could counter interest rate increases that aim to combat inflation, the Organisation for Economic Co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Development said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 In a report coinciding with the run in to Israel's expected entry to the OECD this year, the Paris-based group also called on Israel to work on significantly reducing its public debt burden and combat poverty.\par \par \uc2 OECD chief Angel Gurria said the report, the group's first ever on Israel, "had nothing to do with the accession process."\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, he told Reuters that Israel should become the 32nd member of the OECD by the middle of 2010. [ID:nLDE60I1PH]\par \par \uc2 The report said the economy has weathered the global downturn well and policy responses have been "generally appropriate", with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market helping build international reserves while tempering gains in the shekel.\par \par \uc2 "Nevertheless, a 'clean-float' should be re-adopted as soon as possible," the OECD said. "With the pickup in economic activity, continued discretionary \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  could bring undesirable inflation pressures that work in opposition to any monetary tightening, a process that has begun in recent months."\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Israel in August halted a more than one-year programme of buying $100 million a day of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and instead said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on days of "unusual movements".\par \par \uc2 Such purchases have fallen sharply, with no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in November and just $132 million in December. But the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  numerous times so far in 2010 as the shekel <ILS=> gained some 3 percent against the dollar to a 15-month peak of 3.68.\par \par \uc2 The OECD noted that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  damages transparency and credibility in monetary policy while also bring expensive.\par \par \uc2 And, "if the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aims to provide a floor or ceiling to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , this can easily be thwarted by global markets," it said.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Israel Governor Stanley Fischer told reporters after an OECD forum that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is temporary.\par \par \uc2 "We describe \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a step we hope to stop when we get out of the crisis," Fischer said. "I hope we get to a situation like that. As long as there are differences in interest rates in the world this is an abnormal situation."\par \par \uc2 The OECD praised Israel's government for responsible budget policies and lowering the tax burden to close to the OECD average of 37 percent of gross domestic product. But it cautioned against further tax reductions and said Israel needs to significantly lower its deficits and debt-to-GDP ratio.\par \par \uc2 Its public debt is expected to reach 82 percent of GDP and a goal of falling to 60 percent isn't expected until 2019 assuming real economic growth of 3.5 percent starting in 2011.\par \par \uc2 The report also called for a host of education reforms, such as leveling the educational playing field for Israeli-Arabs and boosting secular training for Israel's ultra-Orthodox population. Both sectors have low labour participation.\par \par \uc2 Gurria estimated the Israeli economy would grow 3-3.5 percent in 2010 and some 4 percent next year after growth of 0.5 percent in 2009.\par \par \uc2 "Growth of 0.5 percent might be small but it's like tying Barcelona -- you go home feeling like it was a victory," he said.\par \par \uc2 OECD-ISRAEL/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|MD|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100120e61k000ay\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
