\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Israel cenbank to raise 2010 GDP f'cast soon -Fischer\b0\par\par\uc2 447 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2010\par 11:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds Fischer comments)\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Jan 7 (Reuters) - The Bank of Israel will soon raise its economic growth forecast for 2010, currently at 2.5 percent, Bank of Israel Governor Stanley Fischer said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "Our current forecast is for gross domestic product growth of 2.5 percent but soon we will revise it upwards," Fischer said in remarks prepared for delivery at a conference. He did not say what the new estimate would be.\par \par \uc2 He noted that after growth of an annualised 3 percent in the third quarter, fourth-quarter GDP growth is expected at 4 percent -- resulting in a "V" picture for Israel's economy.\par \par \uc2 Israel's economy grew an estimated 0.5 percent in 2009, compared with a contraction of 3.5 percent for OECD countries. In 2010, the OECD countries are estimated to grow 1.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 Bank Leumi, Israel's largest bank, on Thursday raised its GDP growth forecast for 2010 to 3.5 percent from a previous estimate of 2.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 Fischer also said that with tax revenues higher than expected, Israel's budget deficit looks to be just above 5 percent of GDP in 2009, below a target of 6 percent. For 2010, the deficit should be lower than a target of 5.5 percent of GDP, he said.\par \par \uc2 As a result of the government's fiscal policies the public debt burden is expected to have grown only slightly, Fischer said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  estimates Israel's debt to GDP ratio at 78.7 percent in 2009 and 78.8 percent in 2010, above 2008's level of 77 percent.\par \par \uc2 The growth of 2 percent since the start of the global crisis is far below the OECD average of 14.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 On the issue of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in an attempt to restrain the shekel's appreciation, Fischer said: "We cannot continue to do this forever".\par \par \uc2 With the shekel gaining to near 3.71 from 3.7750 at the end of 2009, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought as much as $500 million of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  the past few sessions.\par \par \uc2 Fischer partly blames the strong economy and a current account surplus in Israel's balance of payments for the shekel's strength.\par \par \uc2 Israel posted a current account surplus of 3.1 percent of GDP in 2009 and is forecast to record a surplus of 2.3 percent of GDP this year, Fischer said.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Steven Scheer; Editing by Andy Bruce)\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL-FISCHER/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|MD|BNX|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100107e617000pw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
