\par\par \b \uc2 Address by the Governor of the Bank of Israel, Professor Stanley Fischer, to the Conference of the Business Club, Haifa and the North, November 5, 2009\b0\par\par\uc2 1,584 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2009\par \uc2 ENP Newswire\par \uc2 ENPNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009, Electronic News Publishing. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 Release date - 05112009\par \par \uc2 In the past year Israel\u8217\'20\'19s economy underwent a severe crisis that resulted from the worst global crisis since the Great Depression in the last century.\par \par \uc2 The impact on Israel was relatively slight. Although unemployment increased, it did so by less than in large economies such as the US. The feeling currently is that we-both Israel and the global economy-are starting to emerge from the crisis. The impression obtained in the bi-monthly meetings we hold with the BIS \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors is one of recovery. In discussions with them, however, reservations are expressed that derive from the uncertainty regarding the future.\par \par \uc2 Israel\u8217\'20\'19s situation is better than the general situation abroad: no bank has failed, unemployment increased by less than had been expected, and there was renewed growth sooner than expected. We were affected less, because our economy is functioning well, and can cope with crises, as was shown in previous crises: in the last recession the then Minister of Finance and Prime Minister steered the economy out of the crisis, and their actions were essential for the economy-but the Minister of Finance resigned in August 2005. Although the markets reacted immediately with sharp drops, they corrected themselves the next day. The same occurred when Prime Minister Ariel Sharon became ill and could not function, and when the Hamas came into power in Gaza, and on other occasions.\par \par \uc2 Today we have a surplus in the current account, we do not have inflation; the 2007, pre-crisis, budget was almost a balanced one; our banking system is strong; and household debt is lower than that in other countries. These are just some of the examples that indicate the strength of Israel\u8217\'20\'19s economy, and that contributed to Israel\u8217\'20\'19s resilience during the crisis.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Israel also acted to reduce the effect of the global crisis on \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s economy: it cut the interest rate, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, and bought government bonds on the market.\par \par \uc2 1. Cutting the interest rate: we reduced the interest rate sharply. I have heard criticism about the low interest rate, with complaints from some that they have nowhere to invest their money. But that is precisely the purpose of the expansionary monetary policy, to encourage the use of money for consumption and real investments which increase production and create jobs.\par \par \uc2 2. Activity in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) market: at the beginning of 2008 the shekel strengthened greatly, and for very good reasons. As confidence in the markets abroad eroded, Israelis discovered that the shekel was a better and safer alternative, and the repatriation of their investments strengthened the shekel. We in the Bank of Israel took into consideration that a strong shekel in a recession would make the latter more difficult to deal with. We therefore decided that this was a timely opportunity to implement an old plan to increase the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. We started buying $25 million a day, and then increased the amount to $100 million a day. It should be known that the Bank does not follow the shekel/dollar exchange rate only, but closely monitors the effective exchange rate, calculated by weighting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Israel trading partners by their shares of trade with us.\par \par \uc2 3. The purchase of government bonds: this step increased liquidity in the financial system.\par \par \uc2 With regard to the situation today-in mid-November we will receive data on economic activity in the third quarter. Assessments in the Bank of Israel are that the data will indicate positive growth. The Bank\u8217\'20\'19s estimates of growth are zero percent in 2009 and 2.5 percent in 2010. Some forecasters actually expect growth next year to be 3 percent. Although the unemployment rate in Israel is high, our situation is better than that in other countries.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to inflation-last month inflation, measured over the previous twelve months, was within the target range. This month it may be outside the range, above the upper limit, but we must bear in mind that government actions in the field of taxation and surcharges added more than one percentage point to the increase in the CPI; excluding that effect, inflation remains within the target range.\par \par \uc2 Regarding the budget-the government deficit this year is expected to be less than 6 percent of GDP. Government activity in countries undergoing crises increased their debt/GDP ratio; that ratio in Israel will be lower than that in other countries.\par \par \uc2 Housing prices-if someone says that there is a \u8216\'20\'18bubble\u8217\'20\'19 in housing prices I answer that what we have is not a bubble, because the low interest rate reduces the cost of mortgages and encourages purchases of apartments as an alternative investment; it is not a bubble because the prices reflect the economy\u8217\'20\'19s fundamentals. That said, we are keeping a close watch on developments.\par \par \uc2 Regarding the north of Israel-one third of Israel\u8217\'20\'19s GDP is produced there, so that it plays a very important part in total economic activity. During the Second Lebanon War we saw the impact on GDP. The rate of unemployment in the Northern District, however, at 9.3 percent, is higher than in the rest of the country. One of the reasons for this is the lower labor force participation rate in the north. The rate of participation in the central part of the country among those aged 15-64 years is 63 percent, whereas in the Northern District it is only about 51 percent. The gap is the result of the low participation rate among the Arab population in the area, especially Arab women. In the last two years a slight improvement in the participation rate is evident, apparently due to the economic effect of the cuts in allowances, which encourages people to find jobs.\par \par \uc2 The Prime Minister believes that improved infrastructures in the north will boost activity there. I believe that this government will invest more in infrastructures than the previous one, and this will doubtless help to develop the area. The government should play its part in the relevant spheres-infrastructures, defense and public goods-and I believe it will do so. But clearly that in itself is not enough. The continued enterprise and innovation of the people in this hall is essential. Your efforts will be augmented by the recovery of the global economy.\par \par \uc2 Questions addressed to the Governor, and his answers\par \par \uc2 Q. Is there a risk involved in holding the Bank of Israel \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in dollars?\par \par \uc2 A. We hold the reserves in several \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not only in dollars. We manage the reserves very conservatively. The law allows us to purchase only bonds of governments and of sound banks. The managers of the reserves in the Bank of Israel are top rate professionals, and in fact in the latest crisis we did not lose money.\par \par \uc2 Q. What is the Bank of Israel\u8217\'20\'19s view on the huge spread between interest to lenders and borrowers.?\par \par \uc2 A. There are various rates on interest, and one must look at all of them. Thus, for example, interest rates on mortgages are low (prime). Sound and reliable companies from the aspect of their repayment ability pay relatively low rates of interest. When we started cutting the interest rate we urged the banks to lower their interest rates, and they did, but apparently not by enough.\par \par \uc2 Q. We hear forecasts of a second round of the crisis. What is your view?\par \par \uc2 A. There are various forecasts of the future of the economy. Some relate to the letter \u8216\'20\'18V,\u8217\'20\'19 (or \u8216\'20\'18square root sign\u8217\'20\'19) with one low point, and some to the letter \u8216\'20\'18W,\u8217\'20\'19 with two. I think that it is highly probable that there will not be a second round to the crisis, but this is still a probability statement.\par \par \uc2 Q. A Prime Minister once said that \u8216\'20\'18a drought in Israel is of less concern than a drought in the US.\u8217\'20\'19 What is your opinion of the current economic situation? Should we be concerned?\par \par \uc2 A. The effect on Israel of developments in the US certainly exists, but to a lesser extent than in the past. The main question in this context is how the US will exit from its expansionary policy, and what impact will the exit process have on the players in the market. If a cautious approach is adopted, no special problems are anticipated from there.\par \par \uc2 Q. Do you intend to accept the offer to stay on as Governor for another two years?\par \par \uc2 A. The idea of another two years came from Dana Weiss, the presenter of the \u8216\'20\'18Meet the Press\u8217\'20\'19 television program. The government cannot offer a two-year term of office. My current term ends in another six months (less two days).\par \par \uc2 Q. Interest rates are expected to remain low around the world in the next few months. Will the Bank of Israel also keep the interest rate at a low level?\par \par \uc2 A. We will act according to developments in Israel\u8217\'20\'19s economy. In the past we tried to transmit hints of future decisions, but because of the relatively frequent changes in the economy we saw that in all those cases the reality was already different from that on the day we sent out the hints.\par \par \uc2 [Editorial queries for this story should be sent to newswire@enpublishing.co.uk]\par \par \uc2 Dokument ENPNEW0020091112e5bc0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
