\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2 :  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Isn't Tied To Exchange Rate\b0\par\par\uc2 373 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2009\par 14:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Israel doesn't have an exchange-rate target for the Israeli shekel against the U.S. dollar or any other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it said Monday.\par \par \uc2 This statement follows two months of accusations in the Israeli press that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when the shekel appreciates too much against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The bank said it only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when there are "unusual exchange rate movements that do not reflect fundamental economic conditions, or when disorderly conditions prevail in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market."\par \par \uc2 The bank added that it looks at the rate of the shekel not only in relation to the dollar but also other foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and considers multiple macroeconomic factors, not just exchange rates, before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The bank said Aug. 10 it had ended a program it had been carrying out for about a year of daily purchases of $100 million on the open foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in order to increase \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves.\par \par \uc2 However, the bank has continued to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since then. At the end of July, the bank's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stood at $52.4 billion; at the end of September the reserves totaled $59.97 billion.\par \par \uc2 When the bank said in August it would stop its daily $100 million purchases, the shekel was at ILS3.87 to the dollar. That rate has dropped gradually over the following months, fluctuating back up to the Aug. 10 rate only a few times. On Monday the rate was ILS3.708 against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The bank reiterated that its purchases of $7.57 billion in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August and September weren't related to the shekel-dollar rate. It called its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past two months "exceptional, a result of the non-yet ended global crisis."\par \par \uc2 The bank said it hadn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from 1998 through most of 2008, and it hoped to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market once the global economic situation stabilizes.\par \par \uc2 -By Sara Toth Stub, contributing to Dow Jones Newswires; saratoth@gmail.com [ 19-10-09 1205GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091019e5aj00120\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
