\par\par \b \uc2 Israel cenbank says no target dollar-shekel rate\b0\par\par\uc2 465 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2009\par 12:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Oct 18 (Reuters) - The Bank of Israel said on Sunday it did not target a specific dollar-shekel <ILS=> rate, and that its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were exceptional responses to the global crisis to keep the shekel in equilibrium.\par \par \uc2 Since early August, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying dollars on days when it deems there are unusual exchange rate movements that do not reflect fundamental economic conditions or when disorderly conditions prevail in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 That policy followed a more than year-long programme of buying $100 million a day in an attempt to slow an appreciation of the shekel, which was hurting exports, which account for nearly half of Israel's economic activity.\par \par \uc2 In recent days, the market had been expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  should the dollar slip below 3.70 shekels.\par \par \uc2 When the dollar fell below 3.70 shekels last Thursday, the Bank of Israel bought foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , briefly bringing the dollar to 3.72 before moving back to 3.70.\par \par \uc2 Rather than a specific target, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  estimates the equilibrium exchange rate of the shekel, based on the balance of payments of the economy.\par \par \uc2 "These estimates typically change over the course of time as economic conditions change; and by their nature, there is a significant range of uncertainty around estimates of the equilibrium rate," the Bank of Israel said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 "Moreover, deviations from estimated equilibrium exchange rates are common and sometimes persistent."\par \par \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Stanley Fischer has declined to say when the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cease and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would continue to "follow closely" developments in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 "We regard the current period of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as exceptional, a result of the not yet ended global crisis," it said.\par \par \uc2 "We expect that the market will return to a situation closer to that of 1998-2008, when the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market -- or at least, that the need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  will occur only rarely, in exceptional circumstances."\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Israel noted that when it evaluates changes in the shekel, it takes into account not only the rate versus the dollar but also the euro and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It said that since the dollar has weakened globally over the last year, the effective rate of the shekel has appreciated by less than the exchange rate of the shekel.\par \par \uc2 Over the six months ended Sept. 30, the shekel appreciated 10.6 percent against the dollar though the effective rate appreciated only by 3.2 percent, it said. (Reporting by Steven Scheer, editing by Will Waterman)\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL-CENTRALBANK/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|MD|M|D|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091018e5ai00067\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\