\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Israel's Fischer does 'not know' if in rate hike cycle\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven Scheer   \par \uc2 621 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2009\par 11:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds Fischer comments, market reaction)\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Sept 17 (Reuters) - Bank of Israel Governor Stanley Fischer said on Thursday the interest rate increase from last month did not necessarily signal the start of a cycle of monetary tightening.\par \par \uc2 "I simply do not know if it's the start of a process of raising the interest rate. We always will watch economic developments ... and take the decisions when the time comes to do so," Fischer told Israel Radio.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Israel on Aug. 24 became the first major \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise short-term borrowing costs when it increased its key rate by a quarter-point to 0.75 percent.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts expect another rate rise to 1 percent when the Bank of Israel next decides on monetary policy next Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Fischer noted that a rate increase was deemed necessary last month since inflation remained above an annual rate of 3 percent while the economy has started to grow. Minutes of the last meeting showed officials were unanimous in backing a rate increase since the economy had moved to moderate growth, while inflation remained high.\par \par \uc2 The shekel <ILS=> barely moved against the dollar on the remarks, while stock indexes were modestly higher and bond prices were slightly lower.\par \par \uc2 The shekel on Wednesday hit a nine-month peak of 3.74 per dollar. It hovered around 3.75 all session on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The recent appreciation has come even as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  from time to time in the exchange market the past six weeks by buying dollars to try and stem the shekel's advance. In early August, it halted a programme of buying $100 million a day of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favour of random purchases.\par \par \uc2 Fischer said he could not say how long the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 "Despite our \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  there is a lot of fluctuation in the exchange rate," Fischer said.\par \par \uc2 WEAK DOLLAR EVERYWHERE\par \par \uc2 He said the problem is the weak dollar globally, partly caused by a wide U.S. current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 "We hope that after we return to a more normal stage in the world economy, get closer to full employment, we hope and expect to resume a more normal situation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, to something more resembling what was happening the last 10 years," Fischer said.\par \par \uc2 He rejected several proposals that would impose taxes on foreign capital inflows into Israel, saying it failed in the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 Fischer said Israel's economy was starting to recover.\par \par \uc2 "We are in the initial stages of economic recovery and we're still not in a good situation. We will start to emerge from recession when growth begins ... we are on the way to exit," he said, adding Israel was not affected by the global crisis as other countries due to responsible fiscal policies.\par \par \uc2 Israel's economy grew at an annual rate of 1 percent in the second quarter and the Central Bureau of Statistics on Wednesday estimated the economy will grow 0.1 percent in 2009.\par \par \uc2 Fischer said Israel was "technically" out of recession, given the growth in the second quarter. But with Israel's annual growth rate around 1.7 percent, per capita gross domestic product is still negative.\par \par \uc2 And despite big gains in asset and real estate prices, Fischer said he did not see a bubble on both fronts.\par \par \uc2 "We raised interest rates partly in response to the rise in the inflation rate," he said.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Allyn Fisher-Ilan; Editing by Ron Askew)\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL-FISCHER/ (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|MD|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090917e59h000pc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
